    <h1>Second Trimester Guide: Weeks 14-27</h1>

    <p>The second trimester is often called the "honeymoon period" of pregnancy. Many women find that first-trimester nausea fades, energy returns, and they begin to feel the baby move.</p>

    <h2>What Happens in the Second Trimester</h2>

    <h3>Weeks 14-16: Energy Returns</h3>
    <p>Many women feel significantly better in the early second trimester. Nausea usually subsides, and energy levels begin to improve.</p>

    <h3>Weeks 16-22: Quickening</h3>
    <p>Quickening is the first fetal movement the mother feels. In first pregnancies, this typically occurs at 18-20 weeks. In subsequent pregnancies, it may be felt earlier (14-18 weeks) because the mother recognizes the sensation.</p>

    <p>NHS guidance is that you should start to feel your baby move between 16 and 24 weeks, and that early movements can feel like gentle swirling or fluttering.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Your baby's movements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/your-babys-movements/)</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 18-22: Anatomy Scan</h3>
    <p>The anatomy scan is a detailed ultrasound that checks the baby's development, including the brain, heart, spine, and other organs. ACOG recommends at least one standard ultrasound at 18-22 weeks.</p>

    <p>On the NHS it is called the 20-week screening scan, or the anomaly scan. It is usually done between 18 and 21 weeks.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, 20-week screening scan (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/20-week-scan/)</em></p>

    <h2>Your Baby's Development</h2>

    <h3>By Week 14</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby is about 3.5 inches long</li>
      <li>All major organs have formed</li>
      <li>Baby can make facial expressions</li>
      <li>Baby's neck is getting longer</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>By Week 20</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby is about 6.5 inches long</li>
      <li>Baby weighs about 10.5 ounces</li>
      <li>Baby can hear sounds</li>
      <li>Baby's skin is covered with vernix</li>
      <li>You may feel the baby move</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>By Week 24</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Baby is about 12 inches long</li>
      <li>Baby weighs about 1.3 pounds</li>
      <li>Baby's lungs are developing</li>
      <li>Baby has sleep-wake cycles</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Best Start in Life week by week guide to pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/2nd-trimester/week-17/)</em></p>
