    <h2>Common Second-Trimester Symptoms</h2>

    <h3>Back Pain</h3>
    <p>As your uterus grows, your center of gravity shifts, putting strain on your lower back. Good posture, supportive shoes, and prenatal yoga can help.</p>

    <p>NHS advice includes bending your knees and keeping your back straight when you lift, and avoiding heavy lifting.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Back pain in pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/back-pain/)</em></p>

    <h3>Round Ligament Pain</h3>
    <p>Sharp, brief pains in the lower abdomen or groin caused by stretching of the round ligaments that support the uterus. This is normal but can be uncomfortable.</p>

    <p>NHS week-by-week guidance lists pains on the side of the baby bump caused by the expanding womb.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Best Start in Life week by week guide to pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/2nd-trimester/week-24/)</em></p>

    <h3>Pelvic Girdle Pain</h3>
    <p>Pelvic pain in pregnancy is sometimes called pregnancy-related pelvic girdle pain (PGP) or symphysis pubis dysfunction (SPD). It is caused by stiffness of the pelvic joints, or by the joints moving unevenly at the back or front of the pelvis. Pain may be felt over the pubic bone or across the lower back.</p>

    <p>Call your midwife or GP if the pain makes it hard to move around. Physiotherapy can ease pain and improve pelvic joint stability.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pelvic pain in pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/pelvic-pain/)</em></p>

    <h3>Heartburn</h3>
    <p>Growing pressure from the uterus on the stomach can cause acid reflux. Eating smaller meals, avoiding spicy foods, and not lying down immediately after eating can help.</p>

    <h3>Nasal Congestion</h3>
    <p>Increased blood flow to mucous membranes can cause stuffy nose and nosebleeds. This is normal and usually resolves after delivery.</p>

    <h3>Leg Cramps</h3>
    <p>Painful muscle contractions, usually in the calves, often at night. Staying hydrated, stretching before bed, and getting enough calcium and magnesium may help.</p>

    <p>NHS guidance suggests rubbing the muscle hard or pulling your toes up towards your ankle.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Best Start in Life week by week guide to pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/2nd-trimester/week-20/)</em></p>

    <h2>The Anatomy Scan: What to Expect</h2>

    <p>The anatomy scan (also called the level 2 ultrasound) is usually performed at 18-22 weeks. It takes about 30-45 minutes and checks:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Baby's size and growth</li>
      <li>Brain, spine, heart, lungs, kidneys, and other organs</li>
      <li>Placenta position</li>
      <li>Amniotic fluid levels</li>
      <li>Cervical length (in some cases)</li>
      <li>Baby's sex (if you want to know)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The sonographer will take measurements and may ask you to move around to get better views. It is not always possible to see everything in one session.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, 20-week screening scan (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/20-week-scan/)</em></p>

    <h2>Second-Trimester Screening Tests</h2>

    <h3>Quad Screen (15-22 weeks)</h3>
    <p>A blood test that measures four substances: AFP, hCG, estriol, and inhibin A. It screens for Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome, and neural tube defects.</p>

    <h3>Glucose Screening (24-28 weeks)</h3>
    <p>A blood test to screen for gestational diabetes. You will drink a sugary solution, and your blood sugar will be measured one hour later.</p>

    <p>The NHS screening test is an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), which takes about 2 hours. You have a blood test after not eating or drinking for 8 to 10 hours, then a glucose drink, and your blood sugar is measured again later.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Gestational diabetes (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/gestational-diabetes/)</em></p>

    <h3>Hemoglobin/Hematocrit (24-28 weeks)</h3>
    <p>A blood test to check for anemia. Iron needs increase significantly during pregnancy.</p>
    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h2>Vaccinations in the Second Trimester</h2>

    <p>The whooping cough vaccine is always recommended if you are pregnant. It is usually offered around 20 weeks, at the same time as the 20-week screening scan, and you can have it from 16 weeks. You should have it before 32 weeks.</p>

    <p>Babies cannot have their first whooping cough vaccine until they are 8 weeks old, so vaccination in pregnancy passes protection to your baby through the placenta.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Whooping cough vaccine (https://www.nhs.uk/vaccinations/whooping-cough-vaccine/)</em></p>

    <h2>Blood Group and Anti-D</h2>

    <p>Your blood group and rhesus (RhD) status are checked with a blood test at your first antenatal appointment, at around 8 to 12 weeks. RhD status matters when an RhD negative woman is pregnant with a baby who is RhD positive.</p>

    <p>Small amounts of the baby's blood can cross into the mother's bloodstream, for example before birth or after a test such as amniocentesis. The mother's immune system may then make antibodies that can affect a future pregnancy. If there is a risk, you will usually be offered anti-D injections, typically at around 28 weeks and again after the birth.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/rhesus-disease-hdfn/), Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, RhD negative blood type care in pregnancy (https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/-guidance-on-the-use-of-routine-antenatal-anti-d-prophylaxis-raadp-and-postnatal-care-for-rhd-negative-women/)</em></p>

    <h2>Maternity Wear: When to Start</h2>

    <p>Most women need maternity clothes by weeks 14-20, though this varies. High-waisted leggings, empire-waist tops, and layered outfits can extend the life of your regular wardrobe.</p>

<p>Invest in a few key pieces that fit well and feel comfortable. You will wear them for months.</p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if you experience:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Regular contractions before 37 weeks</li>
      <li>Fluid leaking from the vagina</li>
      <li>Severe headache that does not resolve with acetaminophen</li>
      <li>Visual changes (blurring, flashing lights, floaters)</li>
      <li>Sudden swelling of hands, face, or eyes</li>
      <li>Decreased fetal movement after 24 weeks</li>
      <li>A change in your baby's usual pattern of movements</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If you have not felt your baby move by 24 weeks, tell your midwife.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Your baby's movements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/your-babys-movements/)</em></p>

